{"id": "b8666d9e-0905-4532-8a56-7b0c495f23fd", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Here's a bucket policy that grants an IAM role read-only access scoped to a single prefix:

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowListBucketOnPrefix",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/ROLE_NAME"
      },
      "Action": "s3:ListBucket",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME",
      "Condition": {
        "StringLike": {
          "s3:prefix": "PREFIX/*"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "AllowReadObjectsOnPrefix",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/ROLE_NAME"
      },
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:GetObjectVersion"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME/PREFIX/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

Replace the placeholders:
- `ACCOUNT_ID` / `ROLE_NAME` — the IAM role's account and name
- `BUCKET_NAME` — your bucket name
- `PREFIX` — the folder/prefix path, e.g. `logs/2026`

**Notes:**
- The first statement lets the role run `ListBucket` (needed for console browsing / `aws s3 ls`), but only when the request is scoped to that prefix — it can't list the whole bucket.
- The second statement grants actual object reads, scoped by resource ARN to that prefix.
- Drop `s3:GetObjectVersion` if the bucket doesn't have versioning enabled.
- If the bucket uses SSE-KMS with a customer-managed key, the role will also need `kms:Decrypt` permission on that key (separate from this bucket policy).

